00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.880
Why do we need them and
how do they help us?

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.340
So you may have heard
a lot of buzzwords

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.900
associated with containers.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.240
So what we're
trying to do here is

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.270
make you understand why these
are associated with containers.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:40.000
So we'll revisit them
once you've understood

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.340
what containers really are.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.920
So this is the sort of layman's
definition of containers,

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.730
so not very technical but
technically correct still.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.520
So containers is about
two capabilities.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.120
The first thing is, the
container technology

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.480
allows you to package your
application and everything

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.120
that application needs--

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.060
could be the configuration,
any libraries that it needs,

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.880
any runtimes-- like if
you have Java or Python,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.340
it needs a runtime to
run the application--

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.580
so packaging all that
together into one bundle,

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.540
and that bundle is
called an image.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:18.130
So the first capability is the
ability to create that image.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.620
And then the second
part is ability

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:26.230
to run that image as a process
in an isolated environment.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:30.400
So isolated means that
it's sort of cut off

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:36.360
from other processes running on
the same host and the resources

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.260
it can use is limited.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.290
It cannot just run away
with all the resources.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:45.040
So to understand that
a little bit more,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.650
let's look at the
history of containers

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:53.810
as well as how do applications
typically run on a host.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:58.450
So the operating system is a key
component running on a computer

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.310
and it has a number
of components.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.520
The core part of the OS is
called the kernel and that's

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:09.010
the minimum sort of software
you need to run on a computer

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.000
and be able to use it.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.080
Then you need additional--
you might need system modules,

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.950
you might need utilities, tools
that support your application

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.250
or help you manage that machine,
but the kernel is the core.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.500
That's the minimum
thing that you need.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.820
So when the applications
sort of run,

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.350
they are sharing a lot
of these resources.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:31.360
They might be using
shared libraries.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.750
They can bring their own, but
they might be sharing libraries

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.960
with other applications that
are running on the same host.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:42.820
Similarly, they are time-slicing
the resources, the CPU,

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.770
and sharing the memory as well.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:47.680
And if one process runs
away with the memory,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.420
it can starve the
other processes

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.290
running on that machine.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:55.000
So when you're sharing a host
with regular applications,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.500
these are some of the
issues that can creep up.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:01.150
The other thing that can happen
is that the shared libraries--

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:03.430
different applications may
need different versions

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:06.610
of those libraries,
so if one application

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:09.880
changes the library
that's installed,

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:11.870
it can interfere with
other applications.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:15.490
So you have things like, hey,
this ran fine on my machine,

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.680
or this runs fine
in development,

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.050
and you take it to test
and it starts failing

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:23.060
and you can't figure
out what's going on.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:27.020
So to get around that, you
have a number of options.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.250
The first option is--

