00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.240
Let's get stuck
into virtualization.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.180
SHAHID MASUD: Yep.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:24.850
JAMES DUKE: So Shahid
talked just a little bit

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.380
about the kind of
the old school way,

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:28.460
where we have an operating
system and libraries and all

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:29.390
that kind of stuff.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:31.570
The next step on the
journey is virtualization.

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:33.320
So we're not quite at
full containers yet,

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:34.780
but we have the
ability to break up

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.060
a physical machine,
a physical host,

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:38.672
into a number of
virtual hosts, which

00:05:38.672 --> 00:05:40.380
solves a number of
those problems, right?

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.090
We have limits on
CPU and RAM usage.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:43.840
SHAHID MASUD: Yep.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.460
JAMES DUKE: We have
isolation between things.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.720
But there's a lot
of overhead, right?

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:52.577
Because now, as you see here,
four kernels for four VMs.

00:05:52.577 --> 00:05:54.160
It takes a long time
to boot a kernel,

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.270
a kernel uses a lot of overhead,
actual physical resources

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.150
on the host, and
so there is a limit

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:03.670
to how much of the actual
usable computing power

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:08.320
you get out of a machine when
you're using virtualization.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.330
Oh, and they're also
slow to stop because you

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:10.800
have the boot of a kernel.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.720
Booting the kernel
can take a long time,

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.460
so if you want to
spin up a new VM,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.500
these are time
components of that

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.390
which will reduce the speed
at which you can scale.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:20.970
So it becomes a
limit on scaling.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.540
So how do we solve that problem?

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:26.574
Well, one approach
is containers.

00:06:26.574 --> 00:06:28.490
SHAHID MASUD: And so
container is another type

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:30.350
of virtualization, essentially.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.740
What we're doing is
we're sharing the kernel.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:37.670
So we're not dividing the actual
host into virtual machines.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.430
We're just setting up some
boundaries around processes,

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:41.795
and hence the word container.

00:06:41.795 --> 00:06:45.740
We're containing each process
in its own sort of boundary--

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.270
sandbox, if you will--

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:52.070
and using the mechanism
in the kernel to sort of

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:56.030
limit the resources that each
of that process can take.

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.790
So it's sort of a
lightweight virtualization

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:01.070
is the other term
that's used as well.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:05.060
And whatever you do inside
the container stays there,

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:07.580
and any changes that
you make, any libraries

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:09.740
that you bring
into the container,

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:14.600
do not impact any other
processes running on the host.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.950
So let me just show you
guys a running container.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.770
I'll just do a brief demo.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:22.600
So let me just reconnect here.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.280
It seems like I
got disconnected.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:24.580
JAMES DUKE: Classic.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.996
SHAHID MASUD: So
what I'm doing is

00:07:25.996 --> 00:07:29.860
I have a Google Cloud VM
which can run containers,

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:31.810
so I'm just logging in there.

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:33.610
It seemed like my
session timed out.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.470
And what I'll do is I
will run a container

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:39.250
and I'll show you what
that container looks

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:42.760
like from the point of
view of the container

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.890
when you're inside
the container and how

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:48.710
it looks like for the other
processes on the host.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:55.030
So let's just-- so what I'm
going to do is I'm going to use

00:07:55.030 --> 00:08:00.680
Docker, which is a container
runtime, and run up an image--

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.460
the Nginx image-- that you
guys might be familiar with.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:08.530
So what I'm doing here is
I am starting that process.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:12.560
And what you will see is that
that image is now running,

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:15.700
so let's see what that looks
like from the point of view

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:18.460
of the host here.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:20.380
So I'm going to look
at all the processes

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.630
that are running
on this machine,

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:25.480
and what you'll see is that
there are other things running

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.730
there but there's
this Docker daemon

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:31.000
and that is running
the Docker runtime--

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.440
the container
runtime-- and you'll

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:37.480
see that the Nginx is actually
running in that process tree.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.490
And you can see all
the other processes

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:41.240
that are there as well.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.900
Now what I'll do
is I'll just attach

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:46.030
to that container--
what you can do

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:52.690
is you can attach to a
container and essentially start

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:54.580
a shell command
inside the container.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:55.750
So that's what I'm doing.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:59.380
I'm just attaching to that
and executing the bash shell.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.620
So what you'll see is my
command prompt has changed

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:05.350
and now I'm in the
context of that container,

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:08.590
so any commands
that I run here are

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:10.190
running inside that container.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.590
So if I look at
the file system--

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.260
it has its own file system--

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:18.955
and if I run that ps command in
here, what you will see is you

00:09:18.955 --> 00:09:21.250
will see a very limited
set of processes.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:25.510
On the host, you saw that you
had all the other processes

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:28.810
that were running on the host,
but from the container's point

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:31.540
of view, it sees itself
as the only process.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:35.020
So it sees the ps command that
I ran, the shell that I'm in,

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:37.610
and the Nginx, which we
actually wanted to run.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:40.540
So this shows you how the
processes are isolated.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:44.620
So from within the container, it
cannot access any of the other

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:48.630
processes and it's sort
of isolated from those.

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:52.731
So that just shows you what
a running container actually

00:09:52.731 --> 00:09:53.230
looks like.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:01.900
So just to recap quickly the
different ways of running

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:04.909
applications, the traditional
way is shared hosts

00:10:04.909 --> 00:10:06.700
and we saw the problems
with that-- there's

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:09.340
no isolation between
your applications.

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:11.380
Things that one
application does can

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:13.120
impact the other applications.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:16.450
You have to prepare the
host to run the application.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:19.390
Your libraries have to
be a particular version.

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:22.820
If they're not-- if your tools
are not the right version,

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:24.760
you might have issues.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:27.220
Then on the other end, we
have the virtual machines,

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:30.340
which provide a very
high level of isolation.

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:35.140
You can customize each virtual
machine to the exact specs that

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:38.800
you need but the downside
is that it's expensive

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.720
and inefficient and virtual
machines cannot be easily

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:44.270
resized.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:47.560
You cannot increase the size of
a virtual machine that easily

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:50.470
and it's generally a little
bit harder to manage,

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:52.690
but they do provide
very high isolation.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:56.200
Containers provide sort of a
middle ground, where it does

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:58.180
provide the isolation you need.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:01.360
You can install
whatever libraries

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.240
you need inside the container
image, and that way,

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:07.060
your application will
have exactly what it needs

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:08.940
and only what it needs.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.860
You don't want to put a lot
of extra stuff in there.

00:11:11.860 --> 00:11:13.660
And that way,
there's less overhead

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:22.670
and you can use the same host to
host more than one application.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:26.960
JAMES DUKE: It's
always the way--

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.626
the password's always wrong when
someone else is looking, right?

00:11:29.626 --> 00:11:30.640
SHAHID MASUD: Yes.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.020
I had-- yeah.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:36.740
So yeah, so they provide a good
sort of a middle ground, where

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:39.050
they provide the isolation
that you need but they're

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:40.580
nimble and lightweight.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:43.340
So with that, we can revisit
some of the buzzwords

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:46.230
and you'll hopefully understand
it a little bit better.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.300
JAMES DUKE: Yeah, so we
promised at the beginning

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:48.680
to cut through the buzzwords.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:51.675
We just used a whole bunch of
buzzwords in that description,

00:11:51.675 --> 00:11:54.500
so let's revisit them and
really nail them down.

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:56.090
We talked a lot
about lightweight.

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:57.650
So containers are lightweight.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.199
You'll hear this a lot.

00:11:59.199 --> 00:12:00.740
What we're really
talking about there

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.309
is they contain
only what they need.

00:12:02.309 --> 00:12:04.850
So when we spin up the VM, we
had all the extra stuff, right?

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:07.280
We had all the things that
the operating system needs,

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:10.250
the kernel, the dependencies,
libraries which likely are

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:12.140
never used by your application.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:14.660
I can say that doesn't have
any of those things, right?

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:16.220
It just has the bare
minimum you need to run.

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:16.326
SHAHID MASUD: Yes.

00:12:16.326 --> 00:12:16.826
Yep.

00:12:16.826 --> 00:12:20.420
And that provides an extra
benefit that you don't have any

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:23.500
tools that-- if someone--

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:24.470
to be misused.

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:26.520
It reduces the attack surface.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:31.610
So if you had some rogue process
running in your container,

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.190
it has less tools
at its disposal

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:37.185
to wreak havoc in your VM.

00:12:37.185 --> 00:12:38.810
JAMES DUKE: And at
a very simple level,

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:41.951
it uses less resources, which
means you can run more of them.

00:12:41.951 --> 00:12:42.450
Right?

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.637
You can run more containers
on a given physical host

00:12:45.637 --> 00:12:47.553
than you can run VMs on
a given physical host.

00:12:47.553 --> 00:12:48.462
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:12:48.462 --> 00:12:49.920
JAMES DUKE: They
are also portable.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:51.180
You can move them around easily.

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:51.290
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:52.480
JAMES DUKE: Right?

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:52.640
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:54.920
And we'll show you that once
you've built your image,

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:56.190
you've done the hard part.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.460
And that image is very portable.

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:01.070
Since the image contains
everything you need,

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:04.190
all you really need is
sort of a host to run it.

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:06.740
So you need compute and
memory and you can run it

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:08.284
pretty much anywhere.

00:13:08.284 --> 00:13:10.700
JAMES DUKE: And we're going
to focus very much on Google's

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:12.530
platforms today but
that's true, of course,

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:13.160
of all sorts of things.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:13.490
Right?

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:14.990
You can pick up an
image and move it

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.530
to another host, another
platform, another provider.

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:21.380
It just has the ability to
be moved around very easily.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:23.800
And partially because
of the lightweight,

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:25.520
they're fast, right?

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.284
I said earlier,
overhead from a VM,

00:13:27.284 --> 00:13:28.950
you have to wait for
the kernel to boot,

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:30.390
which can take a long time.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:31.640
None of that with a container.

00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:33.080
You start a process,
that's how long it

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:33.980
takes to boot the container.

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:36.063
Plus, of course, anything
that the container needs

00:13:36.063 --> 00:13:38.900
to do for startup, of course,
but the actual container

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:41.870
itself runs much, much, much
faster than, say, booting up

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:44.510
a VM, which means when you're
doing that portability,

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:47.660
there's no penalty and overhead
of starting up that machine.

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:49.790
You don't have to wait two,
three minutes, whatever

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:52.850
it might be, to shut
down and restart a VM.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:54.249
It can be just like that.

00:13:54.249 --> 00:13:55.040
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:56.960
So you can scale
up faster as well.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:59.070
Since you can
share the host, you

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:03.200
can run multiple applications,
so a new host does not

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:04.940
necessarily have to be spun up.

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:07.820
You can use the same host and
quickly start new processes

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:08.990
to take on additional load.

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:10.046
Yep.

00:14:10.046 --> 00:14:11.420
JAMES DUKE: That's
the buzzwords.

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:12.230
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:13.340
JAMES DUKE: Make sense?

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:14.030
Cut through?

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:17.750
Do you feel sufficiently
cut through those buzzwords?

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:18.410
Excellent.

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:19.280
Good stuff.

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:20.500
SHAHID MASUD: All right.

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:22.580
So let's see what it
means for your business.

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:24.290
Why do you want
to run containers?

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:27.380
Now that you have a basic
understanding of what

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:29.310
container technology is.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:34.030
So we see containers as sort
of an enabling technology.

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:36.440
The benefits will
come after you've

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:37.880
started using containers.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:40.500
It's sort of a price of
admission, if you will,

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:43.520
or price you have to
pay to be able to use

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:44.690
a lot of the other things.

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:46.670
And once you've
containerized, you

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:49.530
have a wide variety of
things available to you.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:54.290
You can deploy containers
on bare VMs, what is also

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:56.270
known as infrastructure
as a service,

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:59.870
or you can move to a more
managed service like Kubernetes

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:03.200
Engine, which is typically known
as a platform as a service,

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:06.890
or you can go to App Engine
and serverless offerings that

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:09.270
are now showing up
in the marketplace.

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:12.470
So it allows you to move from
one to the other pretty much

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:13.649
seamlessly and effortlessly.

00:15:13.649 --> 00:15:15.940
JAMES DUKE: I think more what
we're sort of saying here

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:18.680
is, all those things that we
just said a moment ago that is

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:21.420
what containers are, those are
the business benefits, right?

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.420
Fast, portable, lightweight.

00:15:23.420 --> 00:15:25.640
They enable you to do
more things that you just

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:26.750
could not do before.

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:27.250
Right?

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:29.870
So containerization
in and of itself

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:32.090
isn't necessarily the end goal.

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.500
And we actually see this a
lot with our customers, right?

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:34.580
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:35.660
JAMES DUKE: We have people
who want to come to us

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:38.000
and say, well, I need to
containerize because it's cool,

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.880
or I need to containerize
because all of my competitors

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:42.200
are containerizing.

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:44.829
Those are not great
motivations to do this,

00:15:44.829 --> 00:15:47.120
because in and of itself, it
doesn't give you anything,

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:47.600
right?

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:49.940
If you took one application
and containerized it, well OK,

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:50.439
great.

00:15:50.439 --> 00:15:52.730
It's now a containerized
version of the same thing.

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:55.820
It's what you can do next that
is where the real business

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:59.796
value is and what it enables you
to be able to do and grow into.

00:15:59.796 --> 00:16:01.170
SHAHID MASUD: Yep.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:03.780
So here are just
some examples of how

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:07.800
you can take the same image and
run it on different products

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:08.790
that Google offers.

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:12.210
And these are completely
portable between one

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:13.052
and the other.

00:16:13.052 --> 00:16:14.760
So you can take the
image, just deploy it

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:17.220
to Compute Engine
with a glcoud command.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:20.720
You can use Kubernetes
kubectl to deploy it

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:23.410
to a Kubernetes Engine cluster.

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:27.466
Or you can deploy the same
image to an App Engine instance.

00:16:27.466 --> 00:16:29.340
JAMES DUKE: So that's
three different hosts--

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.700
three different targets--
for the same image.

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:32.640
It's really that easy.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.098
Three different
commands and you've

00:16:34.098 --> 00:16:36.365
got the same image running
on three different things.

00:16:36.365 --> 00:16:37.990
SHAHID MASUD: So you
have more options.

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:40.100
So what do we do
with those options?

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:42.590
What are the different
choices of runtimes

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:43.820
and what do they mean for us?

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:44.570
JAMES DUKE: Right?

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:45.704
It's a change of problem.

00:16:45.704 --> 00:16:47.120
Before, we had the
problem of, how

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:49.020
do we make the VM
as good as possible?

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:52.595
Now we have a question of,
where do we put the container?

00:16:52.595 --> 00:16:55.000
How do we choose the right
home for that container

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:56.042
to get the maximum value?

00:16:56.042 --> 00:16:57.625
The good news is,
if you get it wrong,

00:16:57.625 --> 00:16:59.840
it's that easy to change
to a different one, right?

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:01.798
Two commands and you're
back to where you were.

00:17:01.798 --> 00:17:04.369
So it's a very low
risk of a decision

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:06.050
to make but it does
mean that we can

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:08.500
use the different advantages
of the different platforms

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:11.060
for the actual business value,
rather than just because it's

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:13.818
what's technically
compatible with what we have.

00:17:13.818 --> 00:17:14.609
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:17:14.609 --> 00:17:18.990
And the way to look at it is,
if you look from left to right,

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:22.290
the options on the left
end of the spectrum

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:24.869
give you more control
but the downside

00:17:24.869 --> 00:17:28.020
is they come with the
overhead of you managing

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:29.410
your own infrastructure.

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:31.710
So you have to
manage your own VMs.

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:34.380
And it's sort of an
overhead but that's

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:36.990
what a lot of organizations
are used to so it's

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:39.240
a more familiar world for you.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:40.990
So you can start there.

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:45.570
Start with Compute
Engine and run containers

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:49.110
on Compute Engine, and
then you can graduate

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:50.790
to different other offerings.

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:53.580
As you move along
towards the right,

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.040
the offerings are more managed.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:59.730
That means that you have to
spend less resources managing

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:00.990
those offerings.

00:18:00.990 --> 00:18:05.430
And for small organizations,
startups, typically they

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:08.320
don't have the extra resources
to manage the infrastructure,

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:12.030
so that's why we see a lot of
startups start on App Engine

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:14.204
and it's geared towards that.

00:18:14.204 --> 00:18:16.620
JAMES DUKE: And I'm sure you've
heard that a lot this week

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:19.620
and at prior Next and
other Google events.

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:21.870
We're forever talking about
freeing up your developers

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.450
to do what's meaningful, right--
where the actual business

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:25.920
impact is.

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:28.200
Keeping the lights
running on a VM probably

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.850
doesn't make your business or
your customer's business money,

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:31.530
right?

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:34.500
What makes the money is building
new innovation-- innovating

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:37.020
new things, creating
new opportunities

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:39.025
for your customers,
for their customers,

00:18:39.025 --> 00:18:41.420
and so freeing up your
developers to do that is great.

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:44.880
And all we're trying to say here
is that, by containerizing, we

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.216
can get even closer to
that dream of minimizing

00:18:47.216 --> 00:18:48.840
the amount of time
and money that we've

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:50.880
spent on these things which
don't generate that business

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:51.380
value.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:52.800
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:56.070
And sort of the
tradeoff is, as you're

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:59.250
moving towards the right,
you're giving up some control.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:01.170
When you're running in
a managed environment,

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:03.420
you have to understand the
environment that you're

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:07.500
operating in and maybe change
your application accordingly

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:09.310
to take advantage of that.

00:19:09.310 --> 00:19:13.950
So even though staying on a
more self-managed platforms

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:18.190
gives you more freedom,
moving towards the right side

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:23.338
gives you sort of more
flexibility and less overhead.

00:19:23.338 --> 00:19:26.030
JAMES DUKE: Exactly.

00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:26.620
OK.

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:27.815
So that's a lot
about what containers

00:19:27.815 --> 00:19:29.180
are, why you should use them.

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:31.550
Now to the true value here--

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:33.080
how do we get there?

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:35.450
How do we go from
nothing to something

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:37.660
in the world of
containerization?

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:39.560
And the key to
this is, it doesn't

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:42.980
have to be everything
at once, right?

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:46.195
There's no sledgehammer that
fixes this walnut, right?

00:19:46.195 --> 00:19:47.570
We want to break
down the problem

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:49.490
into different
components and tackle

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:52.160
each one of those
components one at a time.

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:54.510
And so the first step to
that is, what have I got

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:56.220
and what category
does it fall into?

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:58.410
Inventory and classify.

00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:02.066
Now inventory, as I'm sure you
know, is not straightforward.

00:20:02.066 --> 00:20:03.690
Doing an inventory
of every application

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:07.334
that you run or your customer
runs is very, very difficult.

00:20:07.334 --> 00:20:09.000
So we're not necessarily
suggesting here

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:12.090
that we want to spend all of our
time talking about inventorying

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:14.790
every application right down
to the tiny little ones that

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:17.410
are barely used and
hiding in the corners.

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:19.360
The real meat here is
in the classification.

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:21.960
So find the 80% of
the applications

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:24.750
and then focus in on those
to see which ones of those

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:26.655
we can use in which wave.

00:20:26.655 --> 00:20:28.860
And we basically said there
are three groups, right?

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:32.220
Group one, we're talking
about stateless applications--

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.177
things where we don't
need to remember what

00:20:34.177 --> 00:20:35.260
happened last time around.

00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:39.750
So classic example is web front
ends, some microservices, APIs,

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.000
but anything where
the current request

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:45.300
doesn't need any contacts
from the prior request.

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:49.830
These are great candidates
for containerization.

00:20:49.830 --> 00:20:52.080
Once you've found this group,
this is the first group.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:53.121
This is the first movers.

00:20:53.121 --> 00:20:55.220
This is the thing that
is the easiest to do

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:57.870
and will give you the
most impact straightaway.

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:01.140
So finding this category
is the place where

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.062
you want to be to move quickly.

00:21:03.062 --> 00:21:04.890
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:08.160
The second group is what we're
calling the second movers.

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:10.530
These are applications
that have some state

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:12.060
but the state is temporary.

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:14.720
Even if you lose the
state, it's not a big deal,

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:17.340
like, caching is a
great example of that.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:20.047
When you start a
caching application,

00:21:20.047 --> 00:21:21.630
it doesn't have
anything stored in it.

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:24.600
You build up the cache
as the application runs

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.650
and then it's useful
that you can look up

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:29.820
things that are cached already.

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:32.400
But if you had to
restart it, yes, you

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:36.900
lose the agility of being
able to look up things

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:39.210
from the cache but it's
not the end of the world.

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:41.915
You can restart and start over.

00:21:41.915 --> 00:21:43.790
JAMES DUKE: Yeah, it's
an accelerator, right?

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:43.930
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:46.410
JAMES DUKE: They accelerate
performance but the actual data

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:47.810
loss is not critical.

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:49.510
SHAHID MASUD: Yep.

00:21:49.510 --> 00:21:51.750
So other examples
in this category

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:54.840
would be things that have
like a master/slave component

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.630
sort of thing where one
instance is the master

00:21:57.630 --> 00:21:59.610
and there are separate slaves.

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:01.870
So again, each instance
is not the same.

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:05.470
There is some state that is
sort of unique to each instance.

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:09.230
So those kind of applications
are again a little bit harder

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:12.210
to not containerize
but run effectively

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:14.260
in a containerized environment.

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:16.110
Creating a container
is not as difficult.

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:18.909
The challenge comes from
actually managing it

00:22:18.909 --> 00:22:20.700
in a containerized
environment-- running it

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:22.260
in a containerized environment.

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:24.676
JAMES DUKE: And then there's
group three, the hard group--

00:22:24.676 --> 00:22:25.770
the stateful applications.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:27.810
Now you'll hear
lots of people say

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:32.310
don't run stateful applications
in containerized environments.

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:33.566
It's good guidance.

00:22:33.566 --> 00:22:35.190
It's not because you
can't and it's not

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:37.600
because there's no
value to doing so.

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:40.110
It's because it's hard
and the value is minimal

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:41.550
compared to the other things.

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:43.950
So any time you find one of
these stateful applications,

00:22:43.950 --> 00:22:44.949
that goes in tier three.

00:22:44.949 --> 00:22:46.040
It goes in wave three.

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:46.230
Right?

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:47.100
SHAHID MASUD: Yep.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:47.760
JAMES DUKE: It can be done.

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:48.686
SHAHID MASUD: Yes.

00:22:48.686 --> 00:22:52.380
JAMES DUKE: Some good
examples-- storage, databases.

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:55.500
Containerizing a MySQL
database is not necessarily

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:57.010
the easiest thing in the world.

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:58.680
It can be done but there's work.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:00.547
There's process.

00:23:00.547 --> 00:23:02.880
And if you think about the
reasons for containerization,

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:06.870
you're talking about ease
of scaling, the performance

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:08.370
savings, things like that.

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:10.320
A database doesn't
lend itself as well

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:13.020
as easily to that as something
like web-front end does.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:13.930
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:15.680
JAMES DUKE: And again,
it's very possible.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:16.745
It can be done.

00:23:16.745 --> 00:23:18.870
But it's definitely lower
on the list of priorities

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:23.340
when it comes to investing your
employees' time and your time

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:26.039
in actually building and
changing that application.

00:23:26.039 --> 00:23:26.830
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:28.538
And if you think about
it, you can't just

00:23:28.538 --> 00:23:31.020
instantiate another
database and expect

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.640
it to sort of work seamlessly.

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.620
There's a lot of
work that goes on

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:37.950
to make the application
and other things

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:40.030
work across these
kinds of applications.

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:42.830
So the benefits that you
get from containerization

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:47.640
of ability to scale up quickly
and scale down based on demand

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:52.390
is not easily done with
these kind of applications.

00:23:52.390 --> 00:23:55.012
So that's why we're
saying that this

00:23:55.012 --> 00:23:56.220
would be the lowest priority.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:56.970
JAMES DUKE: At the
end of the talk,

00:23:56.970 --> 00:23:58.470
we'll come back to
some examples of where

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:01.290
you can see this done the right
way that you can go and look at

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:03.970
if you do get to that stage
three, that group three.

00:24:03.970 --> 00:24:05.830
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:07.830
So yeah, so that
was the first part

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:10.480
is to inventory and sort of
classify your applications

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:11.740
into different groups.

00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:13.510
The second part
is, how do you now

00:24:13.510 --> 00:24:16.060
start containerizing
those applications?

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:18.310
So we'll go through
a number of examples.

00:24:18.310 --> 00:24:21.670
And the great thing is
that you could be anywhere

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:22.700
along this journey.

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:25.060
This is typically how
organizations start.

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:26.860
A lot of organizations
might be running

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:29.000
what are call
monolithic applications.

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:31.630
So you have one
sort of application

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:35.350
that has the frontend and the
services, everything, in one

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:36.310
runtime--

00:24:36.310 --> 00:24:38.800
maybe like a Java
Enterprise application

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:41.720
that does everything
is an example of that.

00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:45.400
So those are typically called
monolithic applications.

00:24:45.400 --> 00:24:49.540
And you have a database where
you store some of the state

00:24:49.540 --> 00:24:54.760
and then the monolith runs
both your frontend as well as

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:55.810
the application.

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:58.120
So you can containerize it.

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:01.840
So the part to containerize
is that monolith.

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:06.730
And the positives of that
are this proves the concept.

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:08.830
If you're just starting
on this journey,

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:11.500
it shows you that
you can containerize,

00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:15.060
and without investing a lot
of effort in re-architecting,

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:16.960
you're proving
that you're getting

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:19.180
familiar with the
containerized world

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:22.150
and proving that
you can manage it.

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:25.060
JAMES DUKE: On the other
hand, as mentioned earlier,

00:25:25.060 --> 00:25:26.890
you now just have
the same application

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:27.891
that is containerized.

00:25:27.891 --> 00:25:29.890
It's running in a container
but it's pretty much

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:30.680
exactly the same.

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:34.240
So there's actually minimal
benefit to doing this.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:37.120
The primary piece is
that it's like practice.

00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:38.470
It's an easy win, right?

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:40.690
We can take one thing
and say, oh hey, cool.

00:25:40.690 --> 00:25:42.470
I know how to containerize
an application.

00:25:42.470 --> 00:25:46.360
And there's learning and merit
in doing that in and of itself

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:49.240
but it doesn't really
give you the scalability--

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:52.060
like the chances of that then
becoming very scalable just

00:25:52.060 --> 00:25:55.067
by putting it in a
container is very slim.

00:25:55.067 --> 00:25:56.650
So you haven't got
those full business

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.780
benefits we've been talking
about, but you have proven,

00:25:58.780 --> 00:26:01.000
hey, I know how to do this,
I know the technologies,

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:03.270
I know the tools, which
gives you confidence when

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:04.620
we move on to the next type.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:05.440
SHAHID MASUD: Yep.

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:08.200
So then the next step
along the journey--

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:09.820
and you may already be here--

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:12.460
is, as we mentioned,
that you have

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:15.209
to take out the stateless
portions of your application

00:26:15.209 --> 00:26:16.250
and sort of containerize.

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:18.050
So containerize them separately.

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:19.810
So what we're doing
is this is sort

00:26:19.810 --> 00:26:22.600
of a three-tier design where
you have a frontend separate

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:26.060
from your backend and then
you have the database as well.

00:26:26.060 --> 00:26:28.360
So this might be the
next logical step,

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:31.210
and in this, you
would again do well

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:34.270
to containerize the
frontend and the backend

00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:36.360
separately, in
separate containers.

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:38.500
The box is not meant to
show that you put them

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:39.640
in the same container.

00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:41.740
You put them in
separate containers

00:26:41.740 --> 00:26:43.750
but that's the portion
of your application

00:26:43.750 --> 00:26:47.170
that you should start
looking at containerizing.

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:49.330
And again, the
positives are you can

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:51.220
scale the tiers individually.

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:53.020
If you have a lot of
frontend requests--

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:57.640
people are just maybe browsing
and not interacting with things

00:26:57.640 --> 00:26:59.530
that require backend services--

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:03.670
you can scale that up and down,
independent of the services.

00:27:03.670 --> 00:27:06.337
Again, the drawbacks
are that you're not

00:27:06.337 --> 00:27:07.420
getting the full benefits.

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:09.670
This is still not scalable.

00:27:09.670 --> 00:27:11.710
But the great thing
is you're getting

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:16.510
closer to the microservices
architecture, which

00:27:16.510 --> 00:27:19.150
I guess everybody loves
that and wants to go there.

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:22.630
So the great thing is that
containerization sort of forces

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:25.000
you towards that architecture.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:28.210
So going along this
journey, you'll

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:30.689
end up with a number of
microservices along the way.

00:27:30.689 --> 00:27:32.980
JAMES DUKE: And each of these
application architectures

00:27:32.980 --> 00:27:34.750
we're showing here are
moving increasingly

00:27:34.750 --> 00:27:36.250
away from the
monolith, increasingly

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:38.510
towards the microservices model.

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:41.110
And the idea here is
we show in each stage

00:27:41.110 --> 00:27:43.510
where we can apply that
containerization technology

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:46.280
and how that enables us to
move further down that path.

00:27:46.280 --> 00:27:48.300
So it may be that you
and your customers

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:50.740
have started off at
the pure monolith

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:53.429
or any one of these
architectures down the line.

00:27:53.429 --> 00:27:55.720
What you'd want to say is
there is a journey and a path

00:27:55.720 --> 00:27:58.690
forward that you can enter
in at any of those places.

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:00.160
So this is one, the three tier.

00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:02.050
The next one would
be if you have

00:28:02.050 --> 00:28:03.800
a transitional
microservices architecture.

00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:06.560
So it's like a traditional
three-tier application

00:28:06.560 --> 00:28:08.830
but now some of
those backend pieces

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:10.330
are broken out as
their own service.

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:12.370
So in this particular
example, service A--

00:28:12.370 --> 00:28:16.060
maybe it's authentication, maybe
it's the item library catalog,

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:17.212
whatever it might be--

00:28:17.212 --> 00:28:19.170
has already been broken
off as its own service.

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:21.310
It hasn't necessarily
already been containerized.

00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:23.800
It may be just running
in a VM or a few VMs

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:25.600
or something like that.

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:29.062
That is a great candidate
for containerization.

00:28:29.062 --> 00:28:30.520
We're now talking
about the ability

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:33.310
to take that one service
and scale it up and scale it

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:35.920
down very easily, move it
across different platforms,

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:39.790
reduce overhead, increase
security, et cetera, et cetera,

00:28:39.790 --> 00:28:42.640
and we're creating by doing
this pattern that we can then

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:45.430
use as we move further down
the microservices path.

00:28:45.430 --> 00:28:47.270
You can think of it
as a toe in the water

00:28:47.270 --> 00:28:49.474
of microservices
architecture, all right?

00:28:49.474 --> 00:28:51.390
You don't have to touch
the existing monolith.

00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:53.530
That remains
unchanged so there's

00:28:53.530 --> 00:28:55.220
minimal risk in the application.

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:57.310
So if this is a
business-critical application

00:28:57.310 --> 00:28:59.380
that's actually
generating the revenue,

00:28:59.380 --> 00:29:02.254
there's minimal risk
in doing these changes.

00:29:02.254 --> 00:29:04.420
However, that's a good thing
and a bad thing, right?

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:05.560
Because now we still
have the monolith.

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:06.940
There's presumably a
reason why you want

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:08.231
to move away from the monolith.

00:29:08.231 --> 00:29:10.720
Whatever that reason is,
this doesn't solve, right?

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:12.280
It's a step in the
right direction

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:14.980
but you're still left with those
problems that you had before.

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:15.400
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:17.440
And the other great
thing with this approach

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:20.950
is that now you're not
re-architecting the whole thing

00:29:20.950 --> 00:29:21.790
at once.

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:26.140
Your existing applications
continues to run and be useful

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:28.790
and you're transitioning
slowly to this new architecture

00:29:28.790 --> 00:29:30.760
so you have fewer disruptions.

00:29:30.760 --> 00:29:34.210
And you're learning--
as you're getting

00:29:34.210 --> 00:29:36.140
comfortable in this
new architecture

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:38.650
and the containerized
world, you're learning

00:29:38.650 --> 00:29:42.130
and it's sort of
spread out over time

00:29:42.130 --> 00:29:45.020
so your risk is reduced as well.

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:46.360
JAMES DUKE: Exactly.

00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:49.540
And then the next step
towards microservices, you

00:29:49.540 --> 00:29:50.860
can add an API gateway.

00:29:50.860 --> 00:29:51.610
SHAHID MASUD: Yep.

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:53.770
And then what you can do is--

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:56.800
one pattern that we see that
is very useful if you're

00:29:56.800 --> 00:30:00.340
going in this kind of a journey
is to add an API gateway.

00:30:00.340 --> 00:30:03.010
API gateway sort of
hides the services that

00:30:03.010 --> 00:30:05.530
are at the backend
from your clients,

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:09.314
which includes your frontend
as well as some clients

00:30:09.314 --> 00:30:11.230
might directly be
connecting to your services,

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:15.250
like if you have a mobile
application that runs

00:30:15.250 --> 00:30:16.600
that is not a browser-based.

00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:18.070
It's a native application.

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:21.550
It may need to call services,
and if it goes through the API

00:30:21.550 --> 00:30:27.760
gateway, then it doesn't need
to know exactly how the services

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:29.460
are laid out at the backend.

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:30.835
So it has a number
of advantages,

00:30:30.835 --> 00:30:33.850
so that might be the next
logical step that you introduce

00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:36.730
into your architecture--
add a gateway, which

00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:39.920
will hide the details of the
endpoints of actual services.

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:42.220
And as you move them
around, it doesn't

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:44.620
matter whether the
response is coming

00:30:44.620 --> 00:30:48.130
from your monolith or just the
new service that you've added.

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:51.210
The clients don't have
to know that detail.

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:54.310
And that way, you can easily
change the service endpoints.

00:30:54.310 --> 00:30:57.190
You can introduce
a new service, you

00:30:57.190 --> 00:31:00.160
can move it around,
and so on and so forth.

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:01.750
The drawback here
is that you need

00:31:01.750 --> 00:31:03.880
to build this sort of gateway.

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:05.890
And this is a common
pattern, though,

00:31:05.890 --> 00:31:07.880
that we see in the industry.

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:11.500
So there are a lot of
examples out there and taking

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:12.900
this step shouldn't be daunting.

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:13.990
JAMES DUKE: And it's not
wasted effort, right?

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:14.890
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:31:14.890 --> 00:31:17.860
JAMES DUKE: In the dream
state fully microservices

00:31:17.860 --> 00:31:20.260
architecture, you may still
want to have the API gateway.

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:22.750
So this is not work you're
going to do it and throw away.

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:24.800
It's work which is
useful later on.

00:31:24.800 --> 00:31:26.270
SHAHID MASUD: Yep.

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:27.250
All right.

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:30.130
And then you can
build on that pattern

00:31:30.130 --> 00:31:32.590
and you can scale
out your services.

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:36.310
You can keep taking services
out of your monolith

00:31:36.310 --> 00:31:41.090
and keep putting them into new
service-oriented architecture.

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:44.480
And this is an approach that
a lot of people recommend,

00:31:44.480 --> 00:31:46.490
even if you're starting
with a new application.

00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:49.150
Even if you have a
greenfield application,

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:52.670
there are two schools of
thought in microservices.

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:54.779
Some people believe
that you shouldn't

00:31:54.779 --> 00:31:55.820
start with microservices.

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:58.060
You should build them
as you need them.

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:02.470
As your use cases
are seen, you truly

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:04.450
realize what services you need.

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:07.762
If you sort of theoretically
start with some services

00:32:07.762 --> 00:32:09.220
that you think you
might need, that

00:32:09.220 --> 00:32:10.850
may not be the right division.

00:32:10.850 --> 00:32:13.270
So this sort of
allows you to move

00:32:13.270 --> 00:32:18.130
into this microservices
architecture at your own pace.

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:19.870
JAMES DUKE: And in
the dream world,

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:22.990
this legacy backend
goes away, right?

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:26.410
The dream utopian
future is everything

00:32:26.410 --> 00:32:29.380
to microservice in a full
microservice architecture.

00:32:29.380 --> 00:32:31.810
The reality in the field,
at least that we're seeing,

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:34.120
is that doesn't really work.

00:32:34.120 --> 00:32:37.170
There's always some element,
or usually some element,

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:38.200
that needs to remain.

00:32:38.200 --> 00:32:40.940
Maybe it's some hardware that
you can't move to the cloud

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:42.190
or move to your microservices.

00:32:42.190 --> 00:32:44.246
Maybe there's some
legacy software

00:32:44.246 --> 00:32:46.120
which has some licensing
requirement that you

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.100
can't meet in the new world.

00:32:48.100 --> 00:32:50.800
But what we're talking
here is the 90% to 95%

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:52.570
of the functionality
of the application

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:54.299
moves into these microservices.

00:32:54.299 --> 00:32:55.090
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:59.402
JAMES DUKE: Now does anybody
recognize this pattern?

00:32:59.402 --> 00:33:01.220
Has anyone seen this before?

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:02.110
Does anyone know what
we're talking about?

00:33:02.110 --> 00:33:02.650
Shout out.

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:03.443
Shout out.

00:33:03.443 --> 00:33:05.419
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:05.419 --> 00:33:06.210
JAMES DUKE: Louder.

00:33:06.210 --> 00:33:07.858
My hearing is terrible.

00:33:07.858 --> 00:33:08.786
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:08.786 --> 00:33:09.494
JAMES DUKE: Yeah?

00:33:09.494 --> 00:33:11.290
You've seen this in the field?

00:33:11.290 --> 00:33:13.700
I'm going to go to my plant,
who I know knows the answer.

00:33:13.700 --> 00:33:14.520
AUDIENCE: Strangler pattern.

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:15.390
JAMES DUKE: Strangler pattern.

00:33:15.390 --> 00:33:16.230
Exactly.

00:33:16.230 --> 00:33:17.646
So those of you
who have seen this

00:33:17.646 --> 00:33:19.140
before, the principle goes--

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:21.570
if you have a great
big tree that's

00:33:21.570 --> 00:33:23.370
been standing for
100 years and there's

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:27.240
vines growing around it, each
one of these microservices

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:28.740
is a vine.

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:31.470
Eventually, when the vines are
big enough and complex enough

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:34.260
and strong enough, if the
tree dies and rots away,

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:35.712
the vines remain in its place.

00:33:35.712 --> 00:33:37.670
And it's a really nice
analogy for this, right?

00:33:37.670 --> 00:33:40.270
We build the microservices
around the monolith

00:33:40.270 --> 00:33:42.270
until we have replaced
all of its functionality,

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:45.150
or 95% of its functionality,
at which point

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:46.560
we can shut it down.

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:49.130
Turn it off or turn the
vast majority of it off,

00:33:49.130 --> 00:33:53.116
and we are left with this true
microservices architecture.

00:33:53.116 --> 00:33:54.740
And in our professional
services group,

00:33:54.740 --> 00:33:57.250
in our application
development specialty group,

00:33:57.250 --> 00:33:59.270
we use this all the time.

00:33:59.270 --> 00:34:02.090
This is our go to market, so
to speak, with our customers,

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:03.994
where we will pitch
this idea and follow it

00:34:03.994 --> 00:34:05.660
through with them,
where we can actually

00:34:05.660 --> 00:34:07.640
start to replace
the giant monolith

00:34:07.640 --> 00:34:10.429
with beautiful microservices
architecture built

00:34:10.429 --> 00:34:13.010
around containers with
containerization, Kubernetes,

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:16.190
GKE in most cases, where we
can actually start to remove

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:18.050
and replace that monolith.

00:34:18.050 --> 00:34:22.070
SHAHID MASUD: And this gives you
another sort of insight into,

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:24.500
when you have these
microservices running sort

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:27.469
of independently, you can
choose different products

00:34:27.469 --> 00:34:28.190
to run them in.

00:34:28.190 --> 00:34:31.219
You might run your frontend
in App Engine, for example,

00:34:31.219 --> 00:34:34.219
and run your backend services
in Kubernetes engine.

00:34:34.219 --> 00:34:37.550
You might have some services
deployed as Cloud Functions.

00:34:37.550 --> 00:34:40.909
So it gives you the ability
to sort of pick and choose

00:34:40.909 --> 00:34:44.600
the appropriate technology to
run your actual application in.

00:34:44.600 --> 00:34:46.550
So that's sort of another--

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:47.670
JAMES DUKE: Exactly.

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:48.400
SHAHID MASUD:
--idea around that.

00:34:48.400 --> 00:34:48.659
Yeah.

00:34:48.659 --> 00:34:49.969
JAMES DUKE: And you
get all the benefits

00:34:49.969 --> 00:34:51.590
we talked about in the
microservices architecture,

00:34:51.590 --> 00:34:52.090
right?

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:54.679
Scale different services,
implement new services,

00:34:54.679 --> 00:34:56.270
replace existing ones.

00:34:56.270 --> 00:34:58.970
It really gives you those three
things we talked about at the

00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:01.550
beginning-- the lightweight,
the fast, and the portable.

00:35:01.550 --> 00:35:04.584
Whilst they are
elements of containers,

00:35:04.584 --> 00:35:07.250
they are what we're striving for
in a microservices architecture

00:35:07.250 --> 00:35:07.880
as well.

00:35:07.880 --> 00:35:11.245
The two things I often hear,
our customers will say,

00:35:11.245 --> 00:35:12.620
well microservices
and containers

00:35:12.620 --> 00:35:14.130
means the same thing.

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:17.510
It's not the same but it's
very closely related, right?

00:35:17.510 --> 00:35:19.680
The two go together hand
in hand very nicely.

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:21.180
You can do one
without the other.

00:35:21.180 --> 00:35:22.850
You could do containers
without microservices,

00:35:22.850 --> 00:35:24.766
you could do microservices
without containers,

00:35:24.766 --> 00:35:26.360
but the two when
they're done together

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:27.810
gives you the most value.

00:35:27.810 --> 00:35:28.601
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:35:28.601 --> 00:35:29.180
Yeah.

00:35:29.180 --> 00:35:33.620
And I guess the third
step is to sort of reap

00:35:33.620 --> 00:35:39.110
the benefits of this new
state and enjoy, I guess,

00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:40.580
the microservices architecture.

00:35:40.580 --> 00:35:42.140
JAMES DUKE: Exactly.

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:45.260
So I mentioned earlier that
once we got towards the end,

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:46.910
we would talk
about some examples

00:35:46.910 --> 00:35:49.730
of stateful applications
running in Kubernetes.

00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:52.772
We actually have a whole
bunch of other things.

00:35:52.772 --> 00:35:54.230
So something that
we are seeing out

00:35:54.230 --> 00:35:56.930
in the world with our
customers and with our partners

00:35:56.930 --> 00:36:01.850
is that there is a
large request and desire

00:36:01.850 --> 00:36:06.710
to have example applications
and workshops to go with them so

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:09.290
that a customer or a
business can understand

00:36:09.290 --> 00:36:12.711
how do I do x with GKE.

00:36:12.711 --> 00:36:14.460
And when I say x, I
mean different things.

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:18.980
So how do I run an application
in GKE in production?

00:36:18.980 --> 00:36:21.260
How do I handle
networking in GKE?

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:24.409
How do I run stateful
applications in GKE?

00:36:24.409 --> 00:36:26.450
And the more we've seen
those questions come out,

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:27.825
the more we've
come to understand

00:36:27.825 --> 00:36:31.430
that we needed something from
the professional services

00:36:31.430 --> 00:36:33.890
viewpoint in addition to
everything that we had.

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:36.200
So we came up with
Project Helmsman.

00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:39.010
And I'm very pleased to announce
that today is the first day

00:36:39.010 --> 00:36:40.760
that the Project
Helmsman code is released

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:42.125
into public open-source.

00:36:42.125 --> 00:36:44.000
You can all go check
this out straight after.

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:45.860
In fact, you could check it
out right now if you wanted to.

00:36:45.860 --> 00:36:47.590
Straight after
the talk, you will

00:36:47.590 --> 00:36:49.670
be able to see it
at this URL here.

00:36:49.670 --> 00:36:51.810
The idea is we've come
up with code samples,

00:36:51.810 --> 00:36:53.630
and there's a few
folks sitting here

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:55.280
in the room who have been very
heavily involved with this,

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:56.240
so thank you very
much to you all.

00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:57.050
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:36:57.050 --> 00:36:58.970
JAMES DUKE: We now
have open-source proof

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:01.820
of concepts for all those things
I just mentioned and more.

00:37:01.820 --> 00:37:03.920
We have matching presentations
which are available

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:06.320
to Google's partners
today, with more to come,

00:37:06.320 --> 00:37:10.010
that allow you to walk through
a one-day or half-day workshop

00:37:10.010 --> 00:37:11.040
of any number--

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:12.740
any one of these
particular things.

00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:15.330
So if you or your
customer has a question,

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:18.650
how do I actually use GKE in
production, we have an example

00:37:18.650 --> 00:37:21.237
and we have a workshop
to work through.

00:37:21.237 --> 00:37:22.820
There will be more--
watch this space.

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:25.030
We will be releasing more
and iterating on these

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:27.575
as time goes on, but I
hope that this generates

00:37:27.575 --> 00:37:29.950
a great deal of value for you
and for your customers that

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:33.290
enable you to really start
to use GKE in the way

00:37:33.290 --> 00:37:35.392
